\description{
Estimates out-of-sample predictive performance, by refitting the model with
each fold of the data held out in turn or, for a julia Laplace fit, by
Pareto smoothed importance sampling without refitting.
}
\details{
Works for both backends. On julia, a held-out row is treated as
missing, so the filter passes through it without an update or a
likelihood contribution. For julia fits, \code{parallelFolds = TRUE} runs
up to \code{cores} folds at once, each in a background R session with its
own Julia session, started before the first fold. This needs the
\pkg{future} package. The sessions stay open for the next call, and
\code{\link{ctJuliaKill}('workers')} ends them. Without it the folds run
one at a time on \code{cores} threads. Separate processes are usually
faster, except when each fold takes only seconds. \code{$processes} on the
result says which was used.

Each fold is a separate optimisation, so on a weakly identified model, or
one where leaving out a fold leaves a parameter poorly determined, two
folds (or the two backends) can end at noticeably different raw parameters
with similar likelihoods.

\strong{K fold on an \code{intoverpop = 'laplace'} fit.} The random effects
are integrated out, so what is scored depends on the type of fold. With
row folds, a held-out row is scored with each subject's random effects at
their mode given the fold's training rows, at the fold's parameters. With
subject folds, the out-of-sample quantity is each held-out subject's
Laplace marginal likelihood at the fold's parameters, returned as
\code{outsampleLogLikSubject}, and \code{outsampleLogLikRow} is \code{NA},
because the marginal likelihood does not split into rows. In a multilevel
model, a subject's marginal likelihood is conditional on the rest of its
outermost group, and subjects of one group held out together share it
evenly. \code{insampleLogLik} is the Laplace marginal likelihood at the
estimate for subject folds, and the sum over rows at the full data modes
for row folds.

\strong{\code{casewiseApproximation}.} Each fold's refit is replaced by one
Newton step from the full data estimate, \code{est - V \%*\% s}, where
\code{s} is the score of the held-out data at the estimate and \code{V} the
inverse of the negative Hessian there. This is the
one-step (infinitesimal jackknife) approximation to the refit, and its
error grows faster than the step. A fold's influence,
\code{t(s) \%*\% V \%*\% s}, is the squared length of the step in
posterior standard deviations. Given a number, folds with an influence at
or above it are refitted, so the approximation is used only where the step
is short. \code{foldstepped} says which folds were stepped. On a julia fit
a stepped fold scores only its held-out rows, so its column of
\code{LogLikRowFolds} is \code{NA} elsewhere.

\strong{\code{method = 'psis'}.} Pareto smoothed importance sampling
(Vehtari, Gelman and Gabry, 2017), without refitting. It estimates the
Bayesian leave-one-out predictive density, a subject's likelihood averaged
over the posterior without it. K fold instead scores each fold at its
refitted estimate, so the two can differ, most for a subject that moves
the estimate. There are two levels:
\itemize{
  \item \code{subjectwise = TRUE}: leave out one subject at a time, or in a
  multilevel model one group at a time, since subjects that share a group
  effect are not independent. Each subject's or group's likelihood has its
  random effects integrated out, by the Kalman filter under
  \code{intoverpop = 'augmented'} and by the Laplace approximation
  otherwise. On a sampled fit (\code{optimize = FALSE}, or
  \code{ctFitUncertainty(fit, 'sample')}) the population parameters are
  its posterior draws. On an optimised fit they are drawn from its normal
  approximation (\code{fit$estimate$cov}, so the fit must have one),
  widened by a factor of 1.5, and importance weighted to the posterior. A
  subject or group with a Pareto k above 0.7 is estimated again with
  \code{nsamples} more draws, centred where leaving it out moves the
  estimate, and the estimate with the lower k is kept
  (\code{pointwise$refined}). This is not done for a fit sampled jointly
  over parameters and random effects (\code{intoverpop = 'none'}). When
  the normal approximation itself is poor (\code{proposal_k} above 0.7),
  every estimate is unreliable, and a warning says to sample the fit and
  use its draws. Even without a warning, k is estimated too low from few
  draws, so a value near 0.7, or a posterior you expect to be skewed (an
  outlying subject, a variance near zero, binary indicators), is worth
  rerunning with larger \code{nsamples} or on a sampled fit.
  \item \code{subjectwise = FALSE}, on an optimised
  \code{intoverpop = 'laplace'} fit only: leave out one row at a time,
  conditional on the estimated population parameters, with each subject's
  random effects drawn from the normal distribution the Laplace
  approximation fits at their mode, widened by a factor of 1.5. This is
  leave-one-out of each row's density given the rows before it, which is
  not exact time series leave-one-out, and it ignores uncertainty in the
  population parameters.
}
Draws for which the random effects' mode cannot be found are dropped and
counted. A Pareto k above 0.7 marks an unreliable estimate for that
subject, group or row, for which K fold is the better tool.

\strong{Laplace fits refined by quadrature.} Both methods cross-validate
the Laplace approximation, so for a fit refined with
\code{intoverpop = 'quadrature'} (in \code{\link{ctFit}} or
\code{\link{ctFitUncertainty}}) they start from the Laplace estimate,
\code{fit$laplace$correction$laplace_estimate}, and the folds are not
refined. \code{scoring} says so.

\strong{Fold refits are not checked.} Each refitted fold skips the final
convergence check and Hessian of a full fit (\code{optimcontrol$certify =
FALSE}), which would cost each fold a Hessian. \code{scoring} notes this
whenever folds are refitted.
}
